  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "Decade-long efforts averted energy crisis during war: PM Narendra Modi",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/decade-long-efforts-averted-energy-crisis-during-war-pm-narendra-modi-126111300821_1.html",
      "description": "Prime Minister Narendra Modi on Friday said steps taken in the last decade helped the country to sail through the crisis emerged during the war in the Middle East.\nHe was inaugurating various welfare projects worth over Rs 15,600 crore here, including renewable energy and petroleum infrastructure.\n\"War around Iran and Gulf countries shows how important energy security is. Steps taken for energy security in the last decade helped the country face the crisis with resilience,\" the PM said.\nRegarding the Metro Pink Line, which was dedicated to the public, Modi said it gives better connectivity to people in Bengaluru.\nHe also noted that the Metro rail network increased from 17 km in 2014 to over 100 km in 2026 in Bengaluru.\nOn the occasion, Modi lauded Karnataka Governor Sudha Yadav for serving the nation as the widow of a Kargil war hero.",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-13T18:36:02"
    },
    {
      "title": "PM Modi inaugurates Bengaluru Metro's Pink Line, travels with CM Shivakumar",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/india-news/pm-modi-inaugurates-bengaluru-metro-s-pink-line-travels-with-cm-shivakumar-126111300732_1.html",
      "description": "Prime Minister Narendra Modi on Friday inaugurated the much-awaited Pink Line from Kalena Agrahara to Tavarekere of the Metro Rail project here, built at a cost of around Rs 3,470 crore.\nPM Modi and Karnataka Chief Minister D K Shivakumar together flagged off the new services and travelled on the Metro train. Modi and Shivakumar were also seen buying separate tickets from the counter.\nThe chief minister explained to the PM about the advantage of a double-decker elevated corridor having a metro rail line as well as a flyover to ease traffic congestion in the city.\nGovernor Sudha Yadav, MP Tejasvi Surya and Minister K B Gowda also travelled in the Metro train.\nThe total length of the Pink Line is 21.25 km, comprising 18 stations. While the elevated corridor, which the prime minister inaugurated, has six stations, the underground section will have 12 stations and is still under construction.\nAccording to an official statement, the Pink Line Project from Kalena Agrahara to Tavarekere",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-13T08:58:52"
    }
  ]
